 2. ⚠️ The new red swing states 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: RealClearPolitics , American Presidency Project . (Nebraska's Senate race pits a Republican against an independent.) Chart: Erin Davis/Axios Move over, Wisconsin and Arizona. With Democrats making Republicans sweat in a host of red-state Senate races, a new batch of swing states may be emerging, Axios' Mike Zapler writes. 

 Why it matters: Some of the closest Senate races are in Nebraska, Ohio, Texas, Iowa and even Kansas. Sure, this could be a blip — or a reminder that it's a 50-50 moody nation, even in a bunch of red states. 🖼️ The big picture: Democratic and Republican Senate candidates are running margin-of-error races in red states that not long ago were thought to be forbidding, or even a wasted effort, to many Democrats.

 The Senate races in Kansas and Ohio are tied. So is Nebraska, where independent Dan Osborn is trying to knock off Sen. Pete Ricketts. Democrat James Talarico has consistently led polls in Texas, though that race appears to be tightening as money pours into the state on behalf of Republican Ken Paxton. ✈️ Reliably red Alaska is also a toss-up. President Trump is flying to Wasilla, Alaska, today to campaign for incumbent GOP Sen. Dan Sullivan. Trump won all those states by double digits in 2024. 👀 Zoom in: Republican operatives told Axios they're far from convinced that what's happening now will create a new slate of swing states in the 2028 race for the White House.

 Democrats say better-than-expected performances by their candidates in several red states would at least open the door to an expanded map — with a big "if." "An opening, yes," Obama's former chief strategist, David Axelrod, told Axios. "A guarantee, hardly." 🥊 Reality check: There's no certainty that Democrats will prevail in red states this year, much less set a path for 2028.

 The polling industry has had enough misses and challenges in recent elections to raise questions about whether we're getting an accurate picture of the political landscape. Keep reading . 

 
 
